Language: en

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:03.870
- Hey, hey, welcome, welcome, welcome in

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:08.370
to another exciting, spellbinding,
action-packed edition

00:00:08.370 --> 00:00:09.203
of "Market Takes."

00:00:09.203 --> 00:00:13.950
We have a just absolutely
packed week of data for you

00:00:13.950 --> 00:00:16.590
coming out this week, but
I am going to tell you

00:00:16.590 --> 00:00:18.570
the three most important things

00:00:18.570 --> 00:00:20.520
that you need to be
watching out for this week.

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:23.790
I am Dion Rabouin, reporter
at "The Wall Street Journal."

00:00:23.790 --> 00:00:25.440
This is "Market Takes."

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:26.970
I know you could have been
anywhere in the world,

00:00:26.970 --> 00:00:29.490
but you're here with me
and I appreciate that.

00:00:29.490 --> 00:00:32.460
Listen, this week, like I
said, we've got a lot coming

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:34.740
but the first thing you're
gonna wanna watch this week

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:36.780
we get the US retail sales report.

00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:38.040
I'm gonna tell you why that's gonna be

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:39.720
so important this week.

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:43.440
Next we get three major reports
on the US housing market.

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:44.760
I'm gonna tell you what
all three of those are,

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:46.200
what to look for in those.

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:47.820
And then we get some earnings reports

00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:51.150
from big name retailers,
Home Depot, Walmart,

00:00:51.150 --> 00:00:53.460
and Target all coming out this week.

00:00:53.460 --> 00:00:56.070
So we are going to hear a
lot about the US consumer,

00:00:56.070 --> 00:00:57.725
the health of the US consumer,
and that's gonna tell us

00:00:57.725 --> 00:01:00.210
about the US economy, spending,

00:01:00.210 --> 00:01:02.130
all of those things because, as you know,

00:01:02.130 --> 00:01:04.620
those are what really
drive the US economy.

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:06.240
So let's get into it.

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:07.500
First things first, let's talk

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:09.720
about the US retail sales report

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:11.970
that is gonna be out on Tuesday for April.

00:01:11.970 --> 00:01:14.520
I wanna put this up here
on your screen right now.

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:16.020
Just give you an idea
what we're talking about.

00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:18.030
Now, as you look over there on the right,

00:01:18.030 --> 00:01:21.660
when we get into 2023 and
those months leading into 2023,

00:01:21.660 --> 00:01:22.980
you see there that we have

00:01:22.980 --> 00:01:25.680
negative month-over-month numbers.

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:27.870
So in November, December,

00:01:27.870 --> 00:01:29.910
you saw retail sales were negative.

00:01:29.910 --> 00:01:32.850
Then January we got a
big pickup up, about 3%.

00:01:32.850 --> 00:01:36.690
And then in February and March
we have seen a decline again

00:01:36.690 --> 00:01:38.220
on those retail sales numbers.

00:01:38.220 --> 00:01:39.540
So that's showing us right now

00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:41.400
that we've got a worrisome trajectory.

00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:44.439
We're seeing a consistent
theme and a consistent pattern.

00:01:44.439 --> 00:01:47.910
As inflation has been falling
along with expectations,

00:01:47.910 --> 00:01:50.040
we are also seeing those
retail sales numbers.

00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:53.160
Again, those aren't pulled
out, those aren't real numbers.

00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:55.860
Those are basically
not inflation adjusted.

00:01:55.860 --> 00:01:59.310
So we are seeing overall
retail sales declining

00:01:59.310 --> 00:02:01.710
month over month for the last five months.

00:02:01.710 --> 00:02:04.200
And if we get another decline in April,

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:06.510
that'll be five out of
the last six months.

00:02:06.510 --> 00:02:09.060
We have seen, again, that
last month was down 1%

00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:12.840
after 0.2% negative the month before.

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:13.890
Economists are projecting

00:02:13.890 --> 00:02:17.430
that we are going to get
a 0.8% increase in April.

00:02:17.430 --> 00:02:19.740
And so that would sort of
buck this trend we've seen.

00:02:19.740 --> 00:02:21.510
But, as you'll see there on your screen,

00:02:21.510 --> 00:02:24.720
consistently we've been negative
for the last five months.

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:27.120
I wanna put another chart
up here on your screen

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:29.880
to just show you what this
looks like year over year.

00:02:29.880 --> 00:02:32.850
And as you can see, just
a consistent decline.

00:02:32.850 --> 00:02:36.000
Ever since we hit that 10% number in July,

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:40.140
retail sales have been coming
down, coming in each month.

00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:43.260
Year over year We're
seeing a declining trend.

00:02:43.260 --> 00:02:44.871
And so we're seeing that
both month over month

00:02:44.871 --> 00:02:47.520
and year over year and
that just illustrating

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:50.010
the overall trend and trajectory

00:02:50.010 --> 00:02:52.680
of retail sales, as in declining spending.

00:02:52.680 --> 00:02:56.370
Spending is pulling back,
consumers not buying as much,

00:02:56.370 --> 00:02:58.380
really getting some of
that inflation fatigue

00:02:58.380 --> 00:03:00.150
even as inflation has been coming down.

00:03:00.150 --> 00:03:01.230
And we are also seeing

00:03:01.230 --> 00:03:04.020
that combined with another
kind of worrisome trend.

00:03:04.020 --> 00:03:06.510
I wanna put another chart
up here on your screen here.

00:03:06.510 --> 00:03:08.790
And this is the US initial jobless claims.

00:03:08.790 --> 00:03:11.099
This is the number of people filing

00:03:11.099 --> 00:03:14.910
for unemployment claims or
saying, okay, I've lost my job.

00:03:14.910 --> 00:03:16.860
I need to get on unemployment,

00:03:16.860 --> 00:03:19.560
requesting for the government
aid in that sector.

00:03:19.560 --> 00:03:22.800
And as you see, in 2023, the
numbers had been going down

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:24.270
but we are seeing a pickup.

00:03:24.270 --> 00:03:26.820
And last week we saw
initial jobless claims

00:03:26.820 --> 00:03:29.460
at the highest since October 2021.

00:03:29.460 --> 00:03:32.640
So the numbers still low broadly.

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:35.850
Overall, we're seeing
still lower than average

00:03:35.850 --> 00:03:37.500
or lower than typical numbers

00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:38.670
on those initial jobless claims.

00:03:38.670 --> 00:03:42.390
But the trajectory, we're
seeing a pickup of the trend

00:03:42.390 --> 00:03:45.690
and, like I said, the highest
in almost two years last week.

00:03:45.690 --> 00:03:48.840
So still low but that
trajectory could be worrisome.

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:50.790
And that's just, that chart
on your screen right there

00:03:50.790 --> 00:03:52.290
is illustrating what we're seeing

00:03:52.290 --> 00:03:54.150
in the initial jobless claims market

00:03:54.150 --> 00:03:56.490
in the unemployment market
that could be worrisome.

00:03:56.490 --> 00:03:58.620
So last thing, let's talk about housing.

00:03:58.620 --> 00:04:01.740
Housing, AKA shelter,
was the biggest component

00:04:01.740 --> 00:04:03.210
of core CPI inflation,

00:04:03.210 --> 00:04:06.450
which picked up more
than expected last month.

00:04:06.450 --> 00:04:08.490
We saw, and we have seen consistently

00:04:08.490 --> 00:04:11.490
over the last few months,
housing has been a major piece

00:04:11.490 --> 00:04:13.260
of the overall inflation picture.

00:04:13.260 --> 00:04:15.270
And this week we'll
get three more reports.

00:04:15.270 --> 00:04:17.580
So we get the NAHB housing market index,

00:04:17.580 --> 00:04:18.720
that's out on Tuesday.

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:21.630
Then we will get housing
starts, that's out on Wednesday.

00:04:21.630 --> 00:04:24.750
And then the big kahuna,
existing home sales on Thursday.

00:04:24.750 --> 00:04:26.520
I wanna put a chart up here on your screen

00:04:26.520 --> 00:04:28.770
to show you what we're
seeing in existing home sales

00:04:28.770 --> 00:04:30.420
and why maybe this could be worrisome.

00:04:30.420 --> 00:04:32.820
And you see a clear pickup

00:04:32.820 --> 00:04:34.980
in the home or the median sales price,

00:04:34.980 --> 00:04:37.290
as in the average price
for an existing home.

00:04:37.290 --> 00:04:38.550
You're seeing a pickup there.

00:04:38.550 --> 00:04:40.620
And that's really what
we've seen all year is

00:04:40.620 --> 00:04:43.830
housing is starting to pick
back up in terms of price.

00:04:43.830 --> 00:04:47.640
That chart just illustrating
a very clear short term

00:04:47.640 --> 00:04:51.720
but a very clear trend of housing prices

00:04:51.720 --> 00:04:53.100
starting to get more expensive.

00:04:53.100 --> 00:04:54.870
We have seen that start to rise again

00:04:54.870 --> 00:04:57.450
after that long-term
decrease that you saw also.

00:04:57.450 --> 00:05:00.180
March marked the first
yearly rent price decrease

00:05:00.180 --> 00:05:03.780
in 36 months, according to rent.com.

00:05:03.780 --> 00:05:05.610
And it followed six consecutive months

00:05:05.610 --> 00:05:07.950
of single-digit increases,
which means we were seeing

00:05:07.950 --> 00:05:11.010
a consistent downturn in
terms of price increases.

00:05:11.010 --> 00:05:15.127
However, we are seeing monthly
rental rates increase 5.3%

00:05:15.127 --> 00:05:17.640
in the last week of March,
according to that same survey.

00:05:17.640 --> 00:05:19.950
So again this is just showing us

00:05:19.950 --> 00:05:21.630
the trend is picking back up.

00:05:21.630 --> 00:05:24.210
And we know and we have seen consistently,

00:05:24.210 --> 00:05:26.677
going back for most of about a year now,

00:05:26.677 --> 00:05:29.580
shelter or housing is a big piece

00:05:29.580 --> 00:05:31.290
of that overall inflation picture.

00:05:31.290 --> 00:05:33.690
And we are seeing housing
prices start to pick back up.

00:05:33.690 --> 00:05:37.800
That is despite a 75%
increase in inventory,

00:05:37.800 --> 00:05:41.280
as in more homes being built,
we are seeing more inventory

00:05:41.280 --> 00:05:43.530
on the shelf, but (laughs) or
more inventory on the shelf.

00:05:43.530 --> 00:05:45.888
We're seeing more inventory in
the market as in more houses

00:05:45.888 --> 00:05:48.300
but that's not bringing the the price down

00:05:48.300 --> 00:05:49.680
because demand is still so high.

00:05:49.680 --> 00:05:52.020
So you'll wanna watch out
for each of those reports

00:05:52.020 --> 00:05:55.020
with the big one being existing
home sales on Thursday.

00:05:55.020 --> 00:05:56.250
Last but not least, we get

00:05:56.250 --> 00:05:59.970
Walmart, Target, and Home Depot
earnings all out this week.

00:05:59.970 --> 00:06:02.430
Home Depot will kick
things off on Tuesday.

00:06:02.430 --> 00:06:04.170
That's big because of
what we just talked about,

00:06:04.170 --> 00:06:06.030
those prices for existing home sales.

00:06:06.030 --> 00:06:09.300
And we are seeing a consistent
increase in home builders.

00:06:09.300 --> 00:06:11.550
Those home builder stocks
have really just been shooting

00:06:11.550 --> 00:06:15.720
up the charts, seeing big gains
for home building companies.

00:06:15.720 --> 00:06:17.760
But is that translating
to the real economy?

00:06:17.760 --> 00:06:19.680
Is that making more jobs for folks?

00:06:19.680 --> 00:06:21.921
Are folks going out and buying
things to furnish these homes

00:06:21.921 --> 00:06:23.610
or is that just an investment boom?

00:06:23.610 --> 00:06:25.800
Is that just folks, you
know, who are delivering

00:06:25.800 --> 00:06:27.090
what was already promised?

00:06:27.090 --> 00:06:30.030
We will learn a lot about that
in those Home Depot earnings

00:06:30.030 --> 00:06:33.240
that we get on Tuesday and
that's gonna tell us a lot.

00:06:33.240 --> 00:06:35.430
Thursday we hear from Walmart,

00:06:35.430 --> 00:06:37.230
that is the biggest retailer in the US.

00:06:37.230 --> 00:06:39.930
That's gonna tell us a lot
about jobs, about spending,

00:06:39.930 --> 00:06:43.170
about how consumers are
doing, grocery, general goods.

00:06:43.170 --> 00:06:44.340
How is Walmart doing?

00:06:44.340 --> 00:06:45.210
And that's gonna tell us a lot

00:06:45.210 --> 00:06:47.790
about how other retailers
are likely performing

00:06:47.790 --> 00:06:50.310
throughout the economy and how
much consumers are spending

00:06:50.310 --> 00:06:51.810
and going out there and buying.

00:06:51.810 --> 00:06:53.790
And then also on Thursday we get Target.

00:06:53.790 --> 00:06:57.180
We'll also get TJX, TJ Maxx,
Burlington, those companies.

00:06:57.180 --> 00:06:59.940
They'll tell us about the kind
of budget-conscious consumer.

00:06:59.940 --> 00:07:01.080
So we are gonna hear from all three

00:07:01.080 --> 00:07:02.490
of those companies this week.

00:07:02.490 --> 00:07:04.050
You're gonna wanna watch out for all those

00:07:04.050 --> 00:07:06.720
for what they tell us about
the market, about consumers.

00:07:06.720 --> 00:07:09.450
And I think the market, the
stock market, the bond market,

00:07:09.450 --> 00:07:10.496
all those markets that we'll talk about

00:07:10.496 --> 00:07:12.360
on "Market Takes" are gonna have

00:07:12.360 --> 00:07:14.520
some major reactions depending
on how these come in.

00:07:14.520 --> 00:07:17.407
So that sets us up, tomorrow
morning, 10:00 AM, be here

00:07:17.407 --> 00:07:20.190
while we unpack the retail
sales report right here

00:07:20.190 --> 00:07:21.180
on "Market Takes."

00:07:21.180 --> 00:07:23.070
I'm your host, Dion Rabouin, reporter

00:07:23.070 --> 00:07:24.210
at "The Wall Street Journal."

00:07:24.210 --> 00:07:25.893
We hope to see you next time.

